        "Why the speed is not the same as in air. Water is far less compressible than air and only about eight hundred times denser, and sound speed goes as the square root of stiffness over density. The stiffness wins, so sound in water is faster, and every wavelength at a given frequency is about four and a half times longer than the one you would work out for the same tone in the room you are standing in.",
